-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/5b1fded9-7f43-4839-8da6-96bbdec8c555
-- title:
--   Signal model: demand uncertainty $\theta$, signals $Y_1,Y_2$, Assumption 1 (corrected)
-- statement:
--   Let $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mu)$ be a probability space. The demand uncertainty $\theta$ and the manufacturers' private signals $Y_1,Y_2$ are real random variables in $L^2(\mu)$, with $E[\theta]=0$ and $E[\theta^2]=\sigma>0$; note that $\sigma$ denotes the variance. The tuple is a **signal model** if $E[(Y_i-\theta)^2]>0$ for each $i$ (the precisions are finite) and
--
--   1. $E[Y_i\mid\theta]=\theta$;
--   2. $E[(Y_1-\theta)(Y_2-\theta)\mid\theta]=0$;
--   3. $E[\theta\mid Y_i]$ is linear in $Y_i$, and $E[\theta\mid Y_1,Y_2]$ is linear in $Y_1$ and $Y_2$;
--   4. $E[Y_i\mid Y_{3-i}]=E[\theta\mid Y_{3-i}]$.
--
--   This is the probabilistic layer of the whole mission: every game-theoretic statement is made on such a model.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Clause 2 corrects the printed Assumption 1(b), $E[Y_1Y_2\mid\theta]=0$, which contradicts $E[Y_1Y_2]=\sigma>0$. Clause 4 is listed as implied on p.8 and is kept as a hypothesis. Manufacturers are indexed by `Fin 2`, so `1 - i` is $3-i$. Conditional expectations are taken with respect to the generated $\sigma$-algebras.
-- source:
--   Jain, Sharing Demand Information with Retailer under Upstream Competition, SSRN:3388957 (PDF of 2021-04-25), §3, pp.7–9, Assumption 1, p.8; precisions finite, p.9

import Mathlib

open MeasureTheory

namespace PushInfoShare

/-- The signal model of Jain, *Sharing Demand Information with Retailer under Upstream
Competition*, SSRN:3388957 (PDF of 2021-04-25), §3, pp.7–9, Assumption 1, p.8.

`θ` is the zero-mean demand uncertainty with variance `σ > 0` (the paper's `σ = 𝕍[θ]` is a
variance, p.7) and `Y i` is manufacturer `i`'s private signal, both in `L²` of the probability
measure `μ`. The clauses are Assumption 1 as used by the paper:

* `unbiased`: (a) `E[Yᵢ | θ] = θ`;
* `cond_uncorrelated`: (b), corrected, `E[(Y₁ − θ)(Y₂ − θ) | θ] = 0`;
* `linear_single`, `linear_joint`: (c), `E[θ | Yᵢ]` is linear in `Yᵢ` and `E[θ | Y₁, Y₂]` is
  linear in `Y₁, Y₂`;
* `cross_forecast`: `E[Yᵢ | Y₃₋ᵢ] = E[θ | Y₃₋ᵢ]`, a relation of p.8 kept as a hypothesis.

**Formalization Note.** Manufacturers are indexed by `Fin 2`, so `1 - i` is the paper's `3 − i`.
The printed (b), `E[Y₁Y₂ | θ] = 0`, contradicts the relations the paper derives from it: they give
`E[Y₁Y₂] = σ > 0`. `noise_pos` is the requirement of p.9 that the precisions are finite; given (a),
`E[𝕍(Yᵢ | θ)] = E[(Yᵢ − θ)²]`. Conditional expectations are Mathlib's `μ[· | m]` for the σ-algebras
generated by the conditioning variables, as a.e. identities. -/
structure IsSignalModel {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
    (θ : Ω → ℝ) (Y : Fin 2 → Ω → ℝ) (σ : ℝ) : Prop where
  measurable_θ : Measurable θ
  measurable_Y : ∀ i, Measurable (Y i)
  memLp_θ : MemLp θ 2 μ
  memLp_Y : ∀ i, MemLp (Y i) 2 μ
  mean_θ : ∫ ω, θ ω ∂μ = 0
  variance_θ : ∫ ω, θ ω ^ 2 ∂μ = σ
  σ_pos : 0 < σ
  noise_pos : ∀ i, 0 < ∫ ω, (Y i ω - θ ω) ^ 2 ∂μ
  unbiased : ∀ i, μ[Y i | MeasurableSpace.comap θ inferInstance] =ᵐ[μ] θ
  cond_uncorrelated :
    μ[fun ω => (Y 0 ω - θ ω) * (Y 1 ω - θ ω) | MeasurableSpace.comap θ inferInstance] =ᵐ[μ] 0
  linear_single : ∀ i, ∃ d : ℝ,
    μ[θ | MeasurableSpace.comap (Y i) inferInstance] =ᵐ[μ] fun ω => d * Y i ω
  linear_joint : ∃ d₀ d₁ : ℝ,
    μ[θ | MeasurableSpace.comap (fun ω => (Y 0 ω, Y 1 ω)) inferInstance] =ᵐ[μ]
      fun ω => d₀ * Y 0 ω + d₁ * Y 1 ω
  cross_forecast : ∀ i,
    μ[Y i | MeasurableSpace.comap (Y (1 - i)) inferInstance] =ᵐ[μ]
      μ[θ | MeasurableSpace.comap (Y (1 - i)) inferInstance]

end PushInfoShare


